<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  THE RELEASE CONFIGURATION. Plaintext HTTP is refused, everywhere, with no exceptions.

  Android has refused cleartext by default since API 28, which is right: the app carries a bearer
  token and a licence photograph, and neither belongs on an unencrypted connection. This file does
  not lift that for anything, and it names no host at all — there is nothing here to get wrong, and
  nothing to review each release.

  The development allowlist — the emulator's alias for its host, loopback, and a laptop on the local
  Wi-Fi — lives in `src/debug/res/xml/network_security_config.xml` and `src/profile/...`, so it is
  merged into debug and profile builds and CANNOT reach a release APK. Android resource merging
  picks the build-type file over this one, so a `flutter run` or a debug build keeps working exactly
  as before.

  Why it moved: a production APK was inspected and found to carry `10.0.2.2`, `localhost`,
  `127.0.0.1` and two `192.168.x.x` addresses in this resource. None of them was reachable — the
  base URL is compiled in as an https:// constant and no code path builds a URL to them — so it was
  inert rather than exploitable. It was still a list of somebody's home network shipped to every
  customer, and a standing invitation for the day someone adds a cleartext fallback and finds it
  already permitted.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" />
</network-security-config>
